# PRIOR-ART REVIEW — cycle 87 dispatch 2, Row D re-run (two edits)

## PART A — THE DIRECTION

### A1 SETTLED ALREADY — re-running a stage recipe on the same bed is settled **in favour**; what is settled *against* is leaving the superseded artefact under the clean stage name

Precedent, same stage family, same bed lineage: `build_d1_m3a3.py` ran **twice** (run 1 → `D1_s3b_m3a3_20260922_075611.vi`, run 2 → the current bed), and run 2 is the DELIVERED Row C (`tools/bench/build_d1_m3a3_run2.log:195-198`). So "re-run the same recipe on the same bed" needs no new authority.

The constraint that *is* on record is the disposal of the superseded file:

> *"RUN 1's ARTEFACT `claudeDev\D1_s3b_m3a3_20260922_075611.vi` … IS REJECTED AND MUST NOT BE USED AS A BED … the rename to `_REJECTED_…` was refused by the permission layer, so it still carries the clean stage name"* (`STATUS.md:26`)

`c87b_plan.md:18-19` re-creates exactly that configuration — two files whose names differ only by timestamp, the superseded one un-renameable — and says nothing about how a later session tells them apart. One line in STATUS naming the 15:36:12 md5 as SUPERSEDED discharges it.

What my citation does **not** cover: `STATUS.md:43`'s *"do NOT re-run the recipe, run 1 is the record"* is scoped to the tunnel-ops recipe (functionally verified 38/38). It is not a general prohibition and does not reach this stage — do not over-apply it.

Process note, not a slug: `stage_d1_m3a3_rowD.py` already ended `BGRUN END rc=1` in cycle 87 (`build_d1_m3a3b_rowD.log:229`-equivalent tail, `:214`+). `CLAUDE.md:267` fires a **firefighter** cycle when the same recipe ends `rc≠0` in two consecutive cycles, and `CLAUDE.md:394` counts renamed recipes as the same recipe. Any single failing gate returns rc=1, so this re-run is one gate-fail away from converting the next cycle to fable/low.

### A3 CONTRADICTED (i) — "a spare node on that diagram shifts addresses" is refuted by the run's own readings

`c87b_plan.md:16-18` and the recipe docstring (`tools/recipes/stage_d1_m3a3_rowD.py:29-32`):

> *"Every remaining address on this build is an INDEX TRIPLE into `Diagram #686`.`Nodes[]`, so a spare node on that diagram is ADDRESS-CORRUPTING, not cosmetic."*

Against it, from the 15:36:12 run:

* the junk node is appended at the **tail** — `nodes_index 27` of 28 (`build_d1_m3a3b_rowD.log:99`), so indices 0…26 are untouched;
* with 28 nodes on the diagram, `#637` still resolved at `nodes_index 4` with uid echo 637 (`:137-138`);
* and on the bed, which already holds 27 nodes on that diagram (see A3(ii)), `Diagram[19].Nodes[21]` echoed `23032 → MATCH` (`:22`).

The purge is still right — §5's *other* two reasons (an unwired-`reference` Invoke is a broken node that pins `ExecState` 0; an unintended object under rule 1a) survive untouched. But the stated *reason to re-run* does not, and it is the reason written into the recipe's own docstring.

### A3 CONTRADICTED (ii) — "the re-run produces a clean bed" / D8's premise: **the bed already carries one unpurged second-pass `Invoke`**

The arithmetic from Row C's delivering run, on the file this dispatch calls the bed:

| reading | value | citation |
|---|---|---|
| cold count at entry | `Node 634` | `build_d1_m3a3_run2.log:33` |
| `Diagram #686` before the write | **26** nodes | `:76`, `:82` |
| junk minted, then purged | 634 → 635 → 634 | `:131`, `:141`, `:143` |
| ordered second pass | `wire_delta 0`, **idempotent ⇒ no purge** (`build_d1_m3a3.py:1432-1437`) | `:174` |
| final in-memory count, then SAVED | **`Node 635`** → `33ef524e…` | `:187`, `:195` |

And the c87 run reading that same file back: `CENSUS BEFORE … 'Node': 635` (`build_d1_m3a3b_rowD.log:86`), `Diagram #686` `nodes_on_diagram` **27** (`:23`).

So the bed is 634+1 and 26+1. The only mutation between Row C's purge and its save was the unpurged idempotent second pass, of the same op family measured at 1.00 stray `Invoke` per call (`tools/stagekit.py:467-468`). Consequences the plan does not account for:

1. **D8 as written cannot see it.** It compares AFTER to BEFORE on the same run, and `junk_purge` only ever deletes nodes new since the last `node_mark` (`stagekit.py:460-474`). The re-run therefore yields an artefact that carries the inherited junk node *and* passes D8. "Clean bed" is not what D8 measures.
2. The failed-prediction review's §5 takes 635 as the clean number (`archive/peer/2026-09-22-c87-rowd-d7-termcount.md:152`: *"the bed is 635, the artefact reads 636"*). On this evidence 635 is already 634+junk, so that sentence understates the defect by one.
3. STATUS NEXT's SECOND ACT wants M4 to reach **COLD `ExecState` 1** — if §5's reasoning about an unwired-`reference` Invoke is right, the inherited node blocks that from inside the bed, not from inside this run's output.

Confirmation is free and read-only, and the review already prescribed the method: `node_labels(diagram 19)` on the bed → 27 rows, one labelled `Invoke Node` with 6 terminals and 0 wired (the same precondition `junk_purge` uses), identified **by shape, never by UID**, which is demonstrably recycled (`archive/peer/2026-09-22-c87-rowd-d7-termcount.md:129`, `:152`).

